ng a condition holds is not itself a derivation. Prefer an already\n91: proved adequate general theorem to rebuilding it. A specialized bridge still\n92: needs its local proof. For E follow [external-source review](external-theorems.md).\n93: For inaccessible text follow Audit A's [retrieval/deferred checks](audit-a.md#paywalled-or-inaccessible-primary-text).\n94: \n95: M is optional. Do not search native declarations for every routine fact before\n96: NL0 review; use precise mathematical reasoning and the background-note policy.\n97: If M is explicitly selected, use the actual pinned declaration as the full source contract, including\n98: its binders, implicit arguments and typeclass premises. Do not insert an\n99: extra condition into that contract or omit a native premise. Keep a specialized\n100: application distinct from the native statement and justify its substitutions.\n101: The probe supplies canonical source metadata; it does not discharge the\n102: application's premises or certify the local proof.\n103: \n104: Write the exact conclusion obtained at an application before using it at a\n105: different domain, measure, carrier or codomain. Any restriction, extension or\n106: identification requires its own available ground. If instead deriving a fact\n107: by a named property's definition, supply that definition and display the\n108: instantiated clause and ensuing reasoning. Do not register a new source just\n109: because a fully displayed definition-based derivation also has a standard\n110: name. Prove each added consequence under all its cases; omit an unnecessary\n111: extra claim rather than leave its proof incomplete.\n112: \n113: Introduce an auxiliary object at its actual first use, with its kind,\n114: existence basis and scope. Keep object dependencies in `objects` and earlier\n115: proof IDs in `from_local`. Preserve the object's type and all uses when\n116: renaming it. Apply `definitions` and the grouped `statement_closure` check to the actual prose.\n117: Use [logical packaging](logical-rules.md) for quantified and conditional work.\n118: \n119: ## 4. Inspect the actual stored candidate\n120: \n121: Use check_draft for structural feedback and read_draft for actual changed\n122: records. Continue from the same-stage candidate after a rejected edit. Stable\n123: unique @ID/@symbol selectors are available in own-stage reads/edits, not in\n124: Audit A evidence pointers. Format work requires a concrete edit or source\n125: query rather than repeated progress reports.\n126: For multiline text use read_draft's numbered line view and its complete-string\n127: hash with replace_lines; avoid retyping an escaped old paragraph merely to\n128: make a small edit. Re-read after a stale hash and use existing @IDs after\n129: array changes. No editor infers missing mathematics.\n130: \n131: Read each own conditional packet after structural checking. It supplies only\n132: that R's actual premises, definitions, fixed logic, output and own argument.\n133: Use the shared criteria to inspect these contents, including each output's\n134: consumer-facing statement, rather than remembering what the full manuscript\n135: or another producer established. This is the same author's inspection, not an\n136: extra independent reviewer and not a second Audit A.\n137: Apply Audit A's shared scope rule: an actual direct N is assumed in this\n138: conditional implication; do not re-prove its producer or read an ancestor\n139: body here. The global graph must still contain that producer, and a needed\n140: fact absent from the exported statement must be established explicitly.\n141: In the existing original_context_note explicitly check the actual D records'\n142: types and guards against their available producers, as well as H/target and\n143: source fidelity. In the inference notes compare each source's exact delivered\n144: conclusion with the next line that consumes it. These are inspections of the\n145: stored proof, not another copy of its argument.\n146: \n147: Record concise located context/output/inference notes using the existing\n148: fields. Their criterion grouping comes directly from Audit A. Report readiness\n149: as no_known_issue, unresolved or not_checked and preserve located open issues.\n150: A complete set of notes with an unresolved issue cannot release the proof.\n151: Resolve it in the actual stored argument, or explicitly justify withdrawing\n152: an erroneous self-finding; reference the resolution location. Do not erase an\n153: old issue by omitting it. Python validates these states and bindings, not the\n154: truth of the author's mathematical judgment. A claim of no_known_issue can\n155: still fail the subsequent independent Audit A.\n156: \n157: ### Routine completion and substantive proof gaps\n158: \n159: A needed statement in the supplied proof is initially a proof obligation:\n160: derive it locally, export a proved N, or supply an exactly verified source.\n161: The fact that the current draft has not done so yet is not a mathematical\n162: obstruction. Missing inventory, source registration or exposition is work to\n163: complete, not by itself a terminal gap report. If reporting an actual obstacle,\n164: identify the concrete implication and the mathematical difficulty encountered\n165: during normal understanding, checking and completion, rather than only noting\n166: that no proof/source has yet been entered. This imposes no fixed time/attempt\n167: count and requires neither a counterexample nor a proof of irreparability.\n168: \n169: When writing NL0, expand the argument along the proof strategy already present\n170: in the original work. Actively supply routine omitted derivations, correct\n171: verifiable typographical errors and non-substantive local mistakes, and write\n172: the complete argument into NL0 without interrupting the user for each item.\n173: Completion must preserve the original target, the original theorem hypotheses\n174: and the core proof strategy.\n175: \n176: If a step remains blocked after normal understanding, checking and completion\n177: attempts, carefully assess the obstacle: is it work needed for ordinary\n178: rigorous expansion, or a substantive gap in the original proof? The model\n179: should make this judgment from the mathematical content and actual progress;\n180: no fixed elapsed-time limit or attempt count is a mathematical criterion.\n181: \n182: If the obstacle is judged to be a substantive proof gap, stop NL0 construction\n183: for the current proof, preserve the completed work, and report the gap's\n184: location, the unsupported implication and the grounds for that judgment.\n185: Do not automatically start new research on the original problem, seek a new\n186: core proof strategy, or modify the original theorem to make the proof pass.\n187: \n188: Recognizing a gap does not require finding a counterexample or proving that\n189: the gap is irreparable. If the only established fact is that completion has\n190: not been achieved, and its substantive nature cannot be judged reliably,\n191: report an \"unresolved suspected proof gap\" rather than declaring the original\n192: theorem false. Prolonged runtime, network or tool faults, and model capability\n193: limitations alone are not evidence of a mathematical error.\n194: \n195: In the isolated NL0 carrier, unfinished source registration, JSON bookkeeping or\n196: ordinary drafting calls for the next actual query, draft or local edit, not an\n197: obstacle report. Keep the own draft and continue within its remaining budget. A\n198: terminal mathematical-gap report must identify a genuinely unsupported\n199: mathematical implication and its grounds, rather than merely restating that\n200: the requested artifact is not finished. A stage outcome is not whole-task\n201: completion; ordinary failed candidates return to bounded correction/review.\n202: \n203: Incorporate completed corrections into the argument and its actual dependency\n204: records. Audit A reviews that revised version; existing hash binding and\n205: invalidation rules still apply.\n206: \n207: \n208: ## 5. Freeze and hand over\n209: \n210: When there is no known unfinished obligation, submit the exact candidate bound\n211: to the author notes and shared acceptance version. If the budget ends first,\n212: preserve the draft as unfinished; do not call a known-incomplete artifact a\n213: complete proof. A diagnostic audit of an unfinished draft, if requested,\n214: remains separate from a qualifying first-acceptance audit.\n215: \n216: Freeze source/graph hashes and generate views and an UNVERIFIED scaffold.\n217: Audit A receives the original problem for fidelity and fresh per-R packets for\n218: local replay; it receives no author notes or prior review feedback. Its actual\n219: judgments determine acceptance under the same shared criteria. Structural\n220: validation, author readiness and mathematical acceptance are distinct results."}
{"path": "nl0/references/self-containment.md", "total_lines": 150, "start": 1, "end": 150, "start_column": 0, "end_column": 74, "requested_end": 150, "has_more": false, "next_start": null, "next_column": 0, "next_end": null, "text": "1: # Self-containment details\n2: \n3: This details the statement/object and dependency/scope requirements grouped\n4: under Audit A's statement_closure and dependency_closure criteria. The\n5: [shared Audit A section](audit-a.md#shared-mathematical-acceptance-requirements)\n6: is the acceptance authority for author and auditor; this annex supplies\n7: interpretation and examples, not an independent permission policy.\n8: \n9: ## Two boundaries\n10: \n11: Accepted NL0 is a complete proof relative to its declared H/E/M roots, frozen\n12: logical foundation and version-bound mathematically reviewed background notes. It is not an outline requiring the original manuscript to\n13: recover missing definitions, formulas, assumptions, or arguments. E/M statements\n14: are fully restated; their primary sources are inspected for provenance, not\n15: used to supply unstated premises. The fixed library's own proof dependencies\n16: need not be recopied. The target work's internal results must be proved here.\n17: \n18: Every R must also be checkable from its packet alone: the complete statements\n19: of its direct inputs, their required definitions/declarations, its complete\n20: output, its local declarations, and its own argument. Another R's proof body,\n21: an ancestor not listed as an input, an ambient original H, an original-source\n22: crosswalk, or remembered material is not an additional mathematical resource.\n23: Whole-target/source-fidelity checks are separate from this local proof check.\n24: Under mathematical-background-v2-1, a reviewer may positively assess routine\n25: local mathematical facts and record precise statements, available conditions\n26: and justification. Exact Mathlib bindings are deferred to nl02lean; no native\n27: query is required for ordinary background. These notes add neither H nor a\n28: core proof route. Mathematical uncertainty still blocks. See\n29: [background notes](background-notes.md).\n30: \n31: Original theorem conditions are H nodes, including conditions that could be\n32: encoded in a refined type. Parameters introduce ambient data/structures, not\n33: an ambient stock of original hypotheses. For example, declare a vector and\n34: put its required nonzero condition in H; do not write “nonzero vector” in its\n35: parameter type and use that condition from an empty input list. The same rule\n36: applies to characteristic/range conditions and equations. An R must list every\n37: original H it actually uses. Standard structure operations/axioms and the fixed\n38: finite-language rules remain available; no new substantive theorem is added.\n39: \n40: An output must be understandable and callable without reading its producer's\n41: argument. Include the actual equalities/inequalities, binder types, hypotheses,\n42: parameter windows, constant dependencies, representatives and a.e./pointwise\n43: meaning. Global defined notation can be expanded from the packet. A formula\n44: number, theorem name, or paragraph location cannot replace mathematical content.\n45: Source locators belong in provenance fields. New mathematical prose uses direct\n46: input IDs and previously proved local F IDs, not inherited numbered references.\n47: \n48: ## Object closure\n49: \n50: For every mathematical object actually appearing in a statement, definition,\n51: local declaration, or proof, locate one legitimate declaration:\n52: \n53: 1. A frozen parameter, explicit logical primitive, or global D definition.\n54:    State its meaning, type/domain and parameter dependence. Global primitives\n55:    cover only core typed syntax, not a dictionary of subject-specific objects.\n56:    New subjects use the same parameter/definition/local declaration mechanism;\n57:    symbol registration never licenses a theorem.\n58: 2. A binder inside the current proposition's full statement. Its scope remains\n59:    that statement. A variable bound in an input does not become a free object\n60:    in the consuming proof; instantiate the statement explicitly.\n61: 3. A local declaration before first use, recording its kind, type, exact\n62:    declaration, introduction basis, and scope. An arbitrary variable requires\n63:    legitimate quantifier introduction; a definition uses already available\n64:    objects; a witness requires an input or an earlier local existence proof.\n65: \n66: A declaration cannot grant existence, uniqueness, invertibility, regularity,\n67: or a desired property merely by naming an object. Those obligations require\n68: proof. Any D guard needed to use the object must be a direct R input, not a\n69: theorem silently recovered from ancestor closure. Record mutually compatible\n70: choices and witness dependence. Temporary assumptions stay inside R and must\n71: be correctly discharged. Branch witnesses, local facts and local notation\n72: cannot escape their scopes. Export a quantified or conditional statement when\n73: needed, rather than a free private name. Global names are reserved; dummy\n74: variables may be reused in disjoint local scopes.\n75: \n76: ## Dependency closure\n77: \n78: Inventory actual factual uses from the prose back to their grounds, not just\n79: from an input list forward to a generic `uses` list. Each substantive imported theorem, prior project lemma or original\n80: assumption must be a direct input. Eligible routine mathematical facts are recorded as mathematical audit notes\n81: with actual local grounds, without requiring a native implementation lookup. A definition supplies meaning, not a theorem. An R output is not its\n82: own premise. Adding the desired formula as an unproved input is not a repair.\n83: \n84: Explicit local calculations and fully proved intermediate facts may remain\n85: inside R. Give a reused local fact a full F statement and identify its earlier\n86: derivation and open assumptions. No separate graph vertex is needed for every\n87: algebraic equality. A nontrivial imported result cannot be called a local\n88: calculation. Substantive reusable lemmas should become N/R pairs; any fact\n89: reused by another R must be exported in a complete proposition statement.\n90: \n91: The controller owns `spec.logic.calculations` and the small core syntax table\n92: (`nl0/scripts/foundations.py`). The clean author tool binds omitted metadata and\n93: rejects extra author permissions or task-specific primitives. This fixed\n94: language grants no MVT, FTC, Fubini, convergence, compactness or regularity\n95: theorem. Such facts need legitimate explicit inputs, an actual local derivation or\n96: eligible positively reviewed mathematical background; the syntax table alone\n97: grants none of them.\n98: An author-written permission claim, in any field or prose, has no authority.\n99: Ordinary typed arithmetic notation is part of this fixed language: natural\n100: numbers, integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers mean their standard\n101: structures with usual numerals and operations. Finite calculations include\n102: their zero/one laws, such as `a+0=a`, as well as explicit algebra/order\n103: derivations. Do not demand a separate E/M for an operation token or an allowed\n104: finite calculation. This does not grant calculus, topology or measure theorems.\n105: Conversely, inspect an actual displayed local derivation before demanding a\n106: duplicate E/M input for its conclusion. A named consequence proved by permitted\n107: finite algebra/order steps is not an imported premise. If a local derivation\n108: fails, locate its unsupported implication rather than its absent theorem name.\n109: \n110: Metadata accounts for the argument but never replaces it. Copying all inputs\n111: into a single `uses` record, registering a symbol without declaring it in the\n112: proof, or writing a PASS label cannot establish closure.\n113: \n114: ## Operational acceptance\n115: \n116: 1. Author using the node template. Reverse-check the actual prose for missing\n117:    declarations, unexpanded references and unlisted facts before freezing.\n118: 2. Generate a packet for each R. The v2 generator supplies no original proof,\n119:    producer proofs, provenance file locators, extra ancestor statements or\n120:    undeclared ambient H. Direct source contracts and required definitions are\n121:    included. Packet hashes bind the mathematical input actually reviewed.\n122: 3. Review all closure/inference checks, admitting positively checked routine\n123:    background notes under the shared policy. Record concrete packet locations and reasons for every verdict.\n124: 4. Perform a fresh local mathematical replay. The reviewer gets the skill instructions\n125:    and that packet, without original proof,\n126:    other R bodies, author review\n127:    notes, old verdicts, or an answer key. Record the actual carrier, packet hash,\n128:    prior exposure and mathematical material read. Skill instructions supply no\n129:    extra mathematical premise. Do not falsely claim a same-context rereading\n130:    is a fresh replay. This rule does not authorize starting a new external\n131:    service or agent; if the required carrier has not been used, keep replay\n132:    UNVERIFIED and report that limit.\n133:    Prior exposure means known task/proof material supplied or read in earlier\n134:    contexts; this is not a claim about unknown pretraining contents. Learned\n135:    mathematical knowledge cannot replace a missing declared premise or proof.\n136: 5. An ineligible missing declaration, missing statement content, unlisted substantive theorem or need\n137:    to look outside the packet blocks acceptance. Record the defect; do not\n138:    silently repair it while assigning PASS. Return substantive/structural defects to authoring and review the frozen\n139:    revision; eligible background notes need no author rewrite. Routine completion does not require a\n140:    separate user interruption for every omission. Distinguish a representation\n141:    defect from a substantive mathematical gap under the existing gap policy.\n142: \n143: The code rejects explicit malformed references, inventories, scopes, guards\n144: and receipts. It does not infer hidden hypotheses from property words in types,\n145: or decide theorem applications by matching a list of mathematical names.\n146: These semantic obligations apply equally to every subject and remain part of\n147: author inspection and Audit A. An omitted inventory entry or unnamed\n148: theorem hidden in prose still requires actual semantic review. Freshness and\n149: truthful reviewer identity need execution evidence; record shape/hashes cannot\n150: authenticate them. No natural-language audit is a Lean kernel certificate."}

