l("beta","bijection Fin(n)→I","S002","S002","witness","Choose a bijection beta by the finiteness and cardinality in F001.",["n","I"],[],["F001"]),
l("mBasis","Z-basis of M indexed by Fin(n)","S002","S002","definition","Reindex bZ by beta to get mBasis_i=bZ_(beta(i)).",["bZ","beta","M","n"]),
l("qBasis","K-basis of U indexed by Fin(n)","S002","S002","definition","Let qBasis be the canonical baseChange(K) of mBasis, with vectors 1⊗mBasis_i.",["mBasis","K","U","n"]),
l("G","unital ring homomorphism End_Z(M)→End_K(U)","S003","S003","witness","Take the homomorphism furnished by E001 at K,M, with G(f)(a⊗m)=a⊗f(m).",["K","M","U"],["E001"]),
l("f0","Z-linear endomorphism of M","S005","S005","arbitrary","Fix an arbitrary Z-linear endomorphism f0.",["M"]),
l("a0","element of K","S005","S005","arbitrary","Take an arbitrary scalar a0 for the pure-tensor verification.",["K"]),
l("m0","element of M","S005","S005","arbitrary","Take an arbitrary lattice element m0 for that verification.",["M"])
],argument:[
s("S001","H003 gives lambda=(ell:Z), so H004 gives dim_C V=n=ell+1. Choose the common integer/complex basis bZ,bC indexed by I from H007. Apply M008 over C to bC: C is nontrivial, and finite-dimensional V is finitely generated, so I is finite. Apply M009 to the same basis: |I|=dim_C V=n. Thus this arbitrary original basis can be reindexed by Fin(n); no specially scaled highest-weight basis of M is assumed.",["H003","H004","H007","M008","M009"],[],["lambda","ell","n","V","M","I","bZ","bC"],[f("F001","The original common basis index I is finite of cardinality n, so a bijection Fin(n)→I exists.",["I","n"])]),
s("S002","Choose beta:Fin(n)→I using F001 and let mBasis be the reindexed Z-basis of M. Its images are still a complex basis. Use the canonical baseChange(K) basis qBasis. M002 identifies qBasis_i=1⊗mBasis_i in U=K⊗_Z M. The structure conditions hold because Z is a commutative ring, K has its integer algebra structure, and M is a Z-module. M005 gives dim_K U=|Fin(n)|=n. Here fields have the strong rank condition, the usual invariant-basis-number consequence of linear independence. Since n=ell+1>0, the index 0 exists. qBasis_0≠0: its own basis coordinate at 0 is 1≠0 in K. Thus U≠0.",["M002","M005"],["F001"],["beta","I","n","mBasis","bZ","qBasis","U","K","M","ell"],[f("F002","U has a K-basis indexed by Fin(n), dim_K U=n=ell+1>0, and U≠0.",["U","K","n","ell"])]),
s("S003","Apply E001 to the field K and the Z-module M, giving a unital ring homomorphism G with G(f)(a⊗m)=a⊗f(m). This is the same pure-tensor formula as D020 F. For each f, both G(f) and F(f) are additive maps, and every tensor is a finite sum of pure tensors, so they agree everywhere. Thus F=G as functions and F itself preserves 1, multiplication, 0 and addition. In particular finite sums, powers, negative operators and integer multiples are preserved. This identifies the prepared adapter with the actual operators used here.",["E001"],[],["K","M","U","G","F"],[f("F003","F is a unital ring homomorphism End_Z(M)→End_K(U), with F(f)(a⊗m)=a⊗f(m) for every f,a,m.",["F","M","K","U"],[["f","Z-linear endomorphism of M"],["a","element of K"],["m","element of M"]])]),
s("S004","The formulas defining S,S_inv are additive in each input and Z-balanced, so their additive tensor maps are well-defined. For any b,a∈K and m∈M, S(b·(m⊗a))=S(m⊗ba)=ba⊗m=b·S(m⊗a); the same calculation proves K-linearity of S_inv. Their composites fix every pure tensor and hence every finite sum of pure tensors. They are therefore mutual inverses. From F002, U≠0; the isomorphism S then gives V_K≠0.",[],["F002"],["S","S_inv","V_K","U","K","M"],[f("F004","S:V_K→U is a K-linear isomorphism with inverse S_inv, and V_K≠0.",["S","S_inv","V_K","U","K"])]),
s("S005","Fix f0 and a pure tensor m0⊗a0. By the defining formulas, S(ind(f0)(m0⊗a0))=S(f0(m0)⊗a0)=a0⊗f0(m0)=F(f0)(S(m0⊗a0)). Equality extends to every tensor by additivity. Thus S ind(f0)=F(f0) S for every f0. If a subspace is invariant under ind(f0), its image under S is invariant under F(f0); conversely use S_inv. The inverse identities also send zero and whole spaces to zero and whole spaces. With F002,F003,F004 this proves N003.",[],["F002","F003","F004"],["f0","a0","m0","M","K","S","S_inv","F","ind","U","V_K"])
],applications:[
ap("M008","S001","R0=C, M0=V, I0=I, b0=bC.",["C is a nontrivial field.","H004 gives finite-dimensional V, equivalently a finitely generated C-module.","The chosen bC is exactly the complex basis furnished by H007."]),
ap("M009","S001","R0=C, M0=V, I0=I, b0=bC.",["Every field has the strong rank condition by the elementary dimension bound on linearly independent families.","H007 gives bC as a complex basis."]),
ap("M002","S002","R0=Z, M0=M, I0=Fin(n), S0=K, b0=mBasis, i0 arbitrary in Fin(n).",["Z is a commutative ring and M is an additive subgroup of V with its integer module structure.","The parameter K is a field and has the canonical Z-algebra structure.","F001 permits reindexing bZ; mBasis is consequently a Z-basis and each index is in Fin(n)."]),
ap("M005","S002","R0=K, M0=U, I0=Fin(n), b0=qBasis.",["K is a field, so its modules obey the strong rank condition (the usual finite linear independence dimension bound).","Fin(n) is finite, and qBasis is the canonical base-changed basis from mBasis, whose values were identified by M002."]),
ap("E001","S003","Instantiate the universally bound source field with K and its Z-module with M; denote its homomorphism witness by G and compare to the explicitly defined F.",["K is the ambient field parameter. M is the given Z-submodule of V and hence an additive commutative group with Z-module structure; no characteristic or freeness condition is required for this adapter."],"A001")
]});
